STEP 2 -- read the proxy CLI surface, read-only                             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: qmark.py types "<prefix> ?" with no carriage return. Its self-test passed each
  time.

awplus(config-radproxy)# ?
      group | nas | proxy | rule | server | source-interface
    proxy ?             acct-port | auth-port | enable
    server ?            A.B.C.D | deadtime | timeout
    server <ip> ?       acct-port | auth-port | key
    nas ?               A.B.C.D ;  nas <ip> ?  ->  key
    group ?             NAME (max 64 letters, numbers, hyphens and underscores)
    rule ?              <1-65535> Rule ID
    rule 1 ?            attribute | realm
    rule 1 realm ?      WORD  Realm pattern to match ('*' is wildcard)
    rule 1 realm <r> ?  group | nostrip | server
    rule 1 realm <r> nostrip ?   group | server
    rule 1 attribute ?  WORD | called-station-id | calling-station-id | nas-identifier
                        | nas-ip-address | user-name
    source-interface ?  A.B.C.D | IFNAME
awplus(config-radproxy-group)# ?      ->  server
    group <name> server <ip> ?            ->  acct-port | auth-port | <cr>

  `realm` and `client` are not commands of this mode. The device answers
  `% Unrecognized command`. Realms are written as `rule N realm`, and the list of allowed
  NAS clients is written with `nas`.

  One side effect to know about: reading the group sub-mode needs the device to ENTER that
  mode, and entering it CREATES `group upstream1` in the running-config. A help probe of a
  mode-entering command is therefore not read-only on this platform. That group is named in
  step 7 and was removed with everything else.
  => STEP 2 PASS: the proxy CLI surface is read, and the commands used in step 3 are
     confirmed to exist.

STEP 3 -- configure the proxy                                               PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Measured limit: `rule N ... server <ip>` cannot reach a server on a non-default port.
  The obvious configuration was refused:

awplus(config-radproxy)# server 10.38.22.33 auth-port 11812 acct-port 11813 key testing456
awplus(config-radproxy)# rule 1 realm example.com server 10.38.22.33
    % Server 10.38.22.33 (1812,1813) is not configured
                                           <-- the rule looked for ports 1812 and 1813 only

  The `rule ... server <ip>` form finds the server by IP address plus the DEFAULT ports,
  1812 and 1813. It offers no way to name other ports. So it can never point at a server
  configured on a different port, even one that is already in the running-config two lines
  above. cfg.py stopped the batch at that "% " line, so `proxy enable` was never sent and
  the DUT was never left half configured.

  The form that works is a server GROUP. A group's own `server <ip> auth-port <n>
  acct-port <n>` does take port numbers:

awplus# configure terminal
awplus(config)# radius-server proxy-server
awplus(config-radproxy)# group upstream1
awplus(config-radproxy-group)# server 10.38.22.33 auth-port 11812 acct-port 11813
awplus(config-radproxy-group)# exit
awplus(config-radproxy)# rule 1 realm example.com group upstream1
awplus(config-radproxy)# proxy enable
awplus(config-radproxy)# end

  cfg.py returned 0 and every command echoed back a clean prompt. The running-config block,
  holding the two deliberately different keys:

    radius-server proxy-server
     server 10.38.22.33 auth-port 11812 acct-port 11813 key jQYFBpWNTbzG9aSAztLu6KUBYDCi9VMQVCj2//jSiBg= encrypted
     group upstream1
      server 10.38.22.33 auth-port 11812 acct-port 11813
     nas 10.38.22.33 key h+EeDXRN2hiSvhQ4iErCYKh+SaDpWqcKIQ88roqWCBw= encrypted
     rule 1 realm example.com group upstream1
     proxy enable

  The `nas` key is `testing123` and the `server` key is `testing456`. Both are stored and
  displayed only in encrypted form.

awplus# show radius proxy-server
                                   Auth  Acct  Auth           Acct
    Server Host/IP Address         Port  Port  Status         Status
    ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    10.38.22.33                    11812 11813 Unknown        Unknown
                                           <-- Unknown until the first exchange
  => STEP 3 PASS: the proxy is configured and enabled, with one NAS, one upstream group and
