    09:42:36 Access-Request         from 10.38.22.34 id=13 user='radclient1' | Message-Authenticator=0xdcec32fcb6e61506a3b3165c979f2d46, User-Name='radclient1', User-Password=<hidden>, NAS-IP-Address=10.38.22.33, NAS-Port=0, NAS-Port-Type=5, Service-Type=1(Login), Proxy-State='173'
    09:42:36 Access-Reject          to 10.38.22.34 id=13 user='radclient1' (wrong password)
    09:42:36 Access-Request         from 10.38.22.34 id=158 user='radclient1' | Message-Authenticator=0x76aeca026c10f7001b0edc6088a9a9d2, User-Name='radclient1', User-Password=<hidden>, NAS-IP-Address=10.38.22.33, NAS-Port=0, NAS-Port-Type=5, Service-Type=1(Login), Proxy-State='65'
    09:42:36 Access-Reject          to 10.38.22.34 id=158 user='radclient1' (wrong password)

  Read P2 carefully, because the result is not what a reader would guess.
    * P2: a realm that matches no rule is STILL proxied, and it is NOT stripped. The
      upstream was asked about `radclient1@other.com`, exactly as sent. So `rule N realm`
      picks the upstream group and controls realm stripping. It does NOT decide whether a
      request is proxied at all. The Reject came from the upstream, not from the DUT, and
      the DUT's log says so: `Login incorrect (Home Server says so): [radclient1@other.com]`.
      Anyone who expects an unmatched realm to be dropped by the DUT would be wrong here.
    * P3: the proxy relayed the upstream's refusal. The meaning is unchanged and the reply
      is re-signed for the NAS.
    * P4: the proxy does NOT detect a wrong NAS secret. It forwards the result anyway. With
      PAP there is nothing in the request that lets the proxy check the key, and the NAS
      sent no Message-Authenticator. So the proxy decrypted the password with its own key,
      got garbage, encrypted that garbage again for the upstream, and the upstream answered
      "wrong password". The Reject is signed with the proxy's real NAS key, and our client
      was using the wrong key, so our client reported the authenticator INVALID. Both halves
      are correct. Together they mean a wrong NAS key cannot be told apart from a wrong
      password here.
  => STEP 5 PASS: all three controls behaved as the design expected. Each Reject on the NAS
     leg has a matching upstream Reject with the same Proxy-State value, so each answer can
     be traced from end to end.

STEP 6 -- `nostrip`: an A/B test on the same request                        PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Two CLI facts found while setting this up. First, `nostrip` goes BEFORE the target, not
  after it:

awplus(config-radproxy)# rule 1 realm example.com group upstream1 nostrip
    rule 1 realm example.com group upstream1 nostrip
                                                                     ^
    % Invalid input detected at '^' marker.

  Second, a rule cannot be edited in place:

awplus(config-radproxy)# rule 1 realm example.com nostrip group upstream1
    % Rule 1 already exists

  So the rule must be deleted and created again:

awplus# configure terminal
awplus(config)# radius-server proxy-server
awplus(config-radproxy)# no rule 1
awplus(config-radproxy)# rule 1 realm example.com nostrip group upstream1
awplus(config-radproxy)# end

  Both "% " lines stopped the cfg.py batch, so nothing was half applied. The measurement
  below was taken only after `show running-config` confirmed the rule had really changed.

  The A/B test. Everything is held the same except the rule.

    A  rule 1 realm example.com group upstream1            (strip, the default)
       NAS sends  radclient1@example.com  ->  upstream receives User-Name='radclient1'
                                              -> Access-Accept  -> NAS gets Access-Accept

    B  rule 1 realm example.com nostrip group upstream1
       NAS sends  radclient1@example.com  ->  upstream receives User-Name='radclient1@example.com'
                                              -> Access-Reject (no such user) -> NAS gets Access-Reject

  The upstream server's log lines for the two halves:

    09:43:57 Access-Request from 10.38.22.34 id=213 user='radclient1'             ... Proxy-State='209'
    09:44:31 Access-Request from 10.38.22.34 id=40  user='radclient1@example.com' ... Proxy-State='108'

  The NAS side of half B:

    === P5 (final): nostrip applied ===
    -> Access-Request id=108 to 10.38.22.34:1812 from 10.38.22.33  User-Name='radclient1@example.com', User-Password=<hidden,16 bytes>, NAS-IP-Address=10.38.22.33, NAS-Port=0, NAS-Port-Type=5, Service-Type=1(Login)
    <- Access-Reject id=108 in 0.004s  authenticator=VALID (responder holds the secret)  Message-Authenticator=0x61049697b7aac0521b35098c681bc042, Reply-Message='Rejected: no such user'
    radsend: sent=1 replies=1 no_reply=0 rejects=1 bad_authenticator=0
    rc=4
  => STEP 6 PASS: the same request from the NAS produced a different User-Name at the
     upstream, and therefore the opposite answer. Only the rule changed. This proves the
     stripping is done by the proxy. It is not an effect of the NAS or of the upstream
     server. The rule was then set back to its stripping form.

STEP 7 -- removal, in two stages                                            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Stage 1: turn the proxy off but leave the rest of the configuration in place.

awplus# configure terminal
awplus(config)# radius-server proxy-server
awplus(config-radproxy)# no proxy enable
awplus(config-radproxy)# end

tb277$ radsend 10.38.22.34 --port 1812 --secret testing123 --user radclient1@example.com --password radpass123 --bind 10.38.22.33 --timeout 4 --retries 1
